[Music]  and now here's something neat that i  wanted you all to see take a look  hi drew my name is fernanda and in  celebration of hispanic heritage month  i'm going to talk to you about the  history behind the iconic katrinas that  are often associated with dia de los  muertos in mexico the first ever katrina  was actually a political cartoon by jose  guadalupe posada and it was published in  1913 during mexican revolutionary times  the original katrina was drawn wearing a  big hat heavy earrings and a gown that  was fashionable for women of the  aristocracy of the time since it was  mexican revolutionary times it was meant  to be commentary on the hypocrisy of the  rich that no matter how big your hat is  how fashionable your clothes are or how  much money you have in the end we are  all just skeletons and this type of  katrina is still recreated by artisans  across mexico and you can really buy  these anywhere fast forward a couple  decades the catherine reemerged in pop  culture after a legendary artist diego  rivera recreated it for one of his most  iconic murals that is mural dream of a  sunday afternoon in alameda park in the  historic center of mexico city he is  painted as a child holding hands with  the katrina and behind him is painted  his famous wife frivolo and over time  these katrinas began to be combined with  the other los muertos a day where we  celebrate life death and life after  death and i personally believe that  these modern day recreations of the  katrina still uphold the same message  that jose guadalupe posada was trying to  tell it's a way of unifying humanity  during dia de los muertos showing that  in the end we are all just skeletons i  hope you enjoyed the little history  lesson drew and i hope to also see you  rocking a catrina for the other los  muertos this year  i hope that you enjoyed that as much as  i did  [Music]

